::ENG4_U06_UA_Q01::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
=A. Use a focused oral claim, accessible slides, credited evidence, and a publication format that fits the audience.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
~B. Treat the speech, slides, and publication as unrelated products.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
~C. Add more information to every slide so the presentation seems complete.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
~D. Ignore source credits if the final product looks polished.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q02::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
=A.  Repetition and parallel structure.#Correct. Clear is repeated and the phrase pattern is balanced.
~B.  Unrelated evidence.#Incorrect. The sentence uses a rhetorical structure, not evidence.
~C.  Sentence fragment only.#Incorrect. The sentence is complete.
~D.  Source citation.#Incorrect. No source is cited.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q03::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
=A. State a focused claim, select credible evidence, explain significance, and acknowledge limits accurately.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, appropriate evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. List every research detail without explaining why it matters.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
~C. Use only personal opinion because oral presentations should sound casual.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
~D. Ignore limitations so the presentation sounds stronger.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q04::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.02
=A. Replace copied paragraphs with concise bullets and speaker notes that explain the evidence.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, appropriate evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. Add unrelated photos so the deck looks more active.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
~C. Keep source paragraphs on slides so the audience can read everything.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
~D. Use smaller font to fit more information onto each slide.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q05::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.02
=A. Define technical terms and reduce dense paragraphs so a mixed audience can follow the message.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. Judge the presentation only by whether the colors look attractive.#Incorrect. Review the lesson and choose the option that best supports purpose, clarity, credibility, access, and publication readiness.
~C. Keep technical terms undefined because advanced vocabulary always improves credibility.#Incorrect. Review the lesson and choose the option that best supports purpose, clarity, credibility, access, and publication readiness.
~D. Remove source citations because they distract from slide design.#Incorrect. Review the lesson and choose the option that best supports purpose, clarity, credibility, access, and publication readiness.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q06::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.03
=A. Match each media element to a clear purpose and make sure all sources are credited.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. Add more media elements even if they repeat the same idea.#Incorrect. Review the lesson and choose the option that best supports purpose, clarity, credibility, access, and publication readiness.
~C. Publish quickly because multimedia products do not need revision.#Incorrect. Review the lesson and choose the option that best supports purpose, clarity, credibility, access, and publication readiness.
~D. Use borrowed visuals without credit if they make the product more attractive.#Incorrect. Review the lesson and choose the option that best supports purpose, clarity, credibility, access, and publication readiness.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q07::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.03
=A. Verify that narration, slides, infographic, research summary, and source credits work together for the audience.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
~B. Add animations before checking source credits or audience access.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
~C. Publish as soon as the product contains several media parts.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
~D. Remove evidence so the product feels shorter and more casual.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q08::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
=A. Focus, evidence, explanation, conclusion.#Correct. This order helps listeners follow the point and support.
~B. Evidence, unrelated detail, focus, conclusion.#Incorrect. The listener needs the focus before evidence.
~C. Conclusion, evidence, unrelated detail, focus.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
~D. Unrelated detail, evidence, conclusion, focus.#Incorrect. This does not support clear oral communication.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q09::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
Presentation excerpt: We need clear evidence, clear reasoning, and clear action. Which device is used? {
=A.  Repetition and parallel structure.#Correct. Clear is repeated and the phrase pattern is balanced.
~B.  Unrelated evidence.#Incorrect. The sentence uses a rhetorical structure, not evidence.
~C.  Sentence fragment only.#Incorrect. The sentence is complete.
~D.  Source citation.#Incorrect. No source is cited.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q10::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
Research brief: A college success office found that 72 percent of surveyed students felt more prepared for interviews after formal presentation practice, and faculty reported that oral explanation helped students clarify claims and evidence. Which option best prepares this research for oral presentation? {
=A. State a focused claim, select credible evidence, explain significance, and acknowledge limits accurately.#Correct. This answer best applies the lesson skill with clear reasoning, appropriate evidence, and audience awareness.
~B. List every research detail without explaining why it matters.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
~C. Use only personal opinion because oral presentations should sound casual.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
~D. Ignore limitations so the presentation sounds stronger.#Incorrect. Review the lesson explanation and choose the option that best connects purpose, evidence, design, and audience.
}

