~C. Publish quickly because multimedia products do not need revision.#Incorrect. Review the lesson and choose the option that best supports purpose, clarity, credibility, access, and publication readiness.
~D. Use borrowed visuals without credit if they make the product more attractive.#Incorrect. Review the lesson and choose the option that best supports purpose, clarity, credibility, access, and publication readiness.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q14::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.03
Publication scenario: A senior team has a one-minute narration, three slides, an infographic, a research summary, and source credits for a public-service announcement about postsecondary training. Which option best supports publication-ready multimedia communication? {
=A. Verify that narration, slides, infographic, research summary, and source credits work together for the audience.#Correct. This answer best applies Unit 6 communication skills with clear reasoning, evidence, audience awareness, and publication readiness.
~B. Add animations before checking source credits or audience access.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
~C. Publish as soon as the product contains several media parts.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
~D. Remove evidence so the product feels shorter and more casual.#Incorrect. Review the Unit 6 lesson content and choose the option that best supports audience, credibility, access, coherence, and professional communication.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q15::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
Which oral response order is strongest for listeners? {
=A. Focus, evidence, explanation, conclusion.#Correct. This order helps listeners follow the point and support.
~B. Evidence, unrelated detail, focus, conclusion.#Incorrect. The listener needs the focus before evidence.
~C. Conclusion, evidence, unrelated detail, focus.#Incorrect. This order is confusing.
~D. Unrelated detail, evidence, conclusion, focus.#Incorrect. This does not support clear oral communication.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q16::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.R.12
Why is the repeated word clear effective in the excerpt? {
~A.  It distracts from the point.#Incorrect. The repetition supports the point.
=B.  It emphasizes the qualities the speaker wants the audience to remember.#Correct. Repetition makes the key idea memorable.
~C.  It replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Rhetoric should not replace evidence.
~D.  It makes the claim unrelated.#Incorrect. The repetition reinforces the claim.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q17::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.R.10
Source note: Workers with unreliable transportation are more likely to miss scheduled shifts. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A. Workers with reliable transportation miss more shifts.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
=B. Unreliable transportation can make workers miss scheduled shifts.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~C. Transportation has no effect on work schedules.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source note.
~D. Workers should avoid all transportation.#Incorrect. This adds an unsupported idea.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q18::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.V.01
Which sentence uses academic vocabulary effectively in an oral response? {
~A. The thing is good because stuff happens.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. Evidence is a word in the presentation.#Incorrect. This does not use academic vocabulary meaningfully.
=C. The evidence is credible because it comes from a local workforce report.#Correct. Credible is used accurately.
~D. The claim is claimy and has evidence things.#Incorrect. This is not academic language.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q19::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.L.01
Which spoken sentence is clearest and most conventional? {
~A. Evidence supports claim because report.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
=B. The evidence supports the claim because it comes from a credible report.#Correct. This is clear and conventional.
~C. The evidence supports the claim it comes from report.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
~D. Because credible report evidence.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q20::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
Which statement best explains why focus matters in oral communication? {
=A. A clear focus helps listeners understand how each piece of evidence supports the main point.#Correct. Focus guides listener understanding.
~B. Focus matters only in writing, not speaking.#Incorrect. Focus is essential in oral communication.
~C. Focus means adding as many topics as possible.#Incorrect. Focus narrows the message.
~D. Focus replaces evidence.#Incorrect. Focus and evidence work together.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q21::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
Which opening is most focused for an oral presentation? {
=A.  Reliable transportation supports employment stability by reducing missed shifts.#Correct. This gives a clear and specific focus.
~B.  I will talk about transportation and maybe jobs.#Incorrect. This is unfocused.
~C.  Transportation is a thing people use.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D.  There are many topics to discuss.#Incorrect. This does not establish a focus.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q22::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.R.10
Source note: A city report links unreliable transit to increased worker absences. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A.  Reliable transit causes worker absences.#Incorrect. This reverses the source meaning.
=B.  A city report connects unreliable transit with more worker absences.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~C.  A city report says absences never relate to transit.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source note.
~D.  Workers are absent because reports exist.#Incorrect. This is illogical.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q23::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.R.08
Which use of rhetoric is weakest? {
~A.  A rhetorical question after evidence has been explained.#Incorrect. That can be effective.
~B.  Parallel structure that organizes three reasons.#Incorrect. That can strengthen organization.
=C.  A dramatic claim with no evidence or reasoning.#Correct. Rhetoric without evidence weakens argument quality.
~D.  Repetition that emphasizes a proven pattern.#Incorrect. That can support evidence.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q24::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.V.01
Which word best describes evidence from a trustworthy public report? {
~A.  decorative.#Incorrect. Decorative does not describe trustworthiness.
~B.  random.#Incorrect. Random does not show reliability.
=C.  credible.#Correct. Credible means trustworthy or believable.
~D.  unrelated.#Incorrect. Unrelated does not describe source quality.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q25::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.L.01
Which sentence uses standard English? {
~A.  The evidence credible because report.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
~B.  The evidence is credible it comes from a public report.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
~C.  Because evidence credible public report.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
=D.  The evidence is credible because it comes from a public report.#Correct. This is clear and complete.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q26::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q26
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
What should come immediately after a focused claim in a short oral response? {
=A.  Credible evidence that supports the claim.#Correct. Evidence should support the focus.
~B.  An unrelated personal story.#Incorrect. This distracts from the focus.
~C.  A conclusion with no support.#Incorrect. The listener needs evidence first.
~D.  A new topic.#Incorrect. This weakens organization.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q27::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q27
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.R.10
~A. Workers with reliable transportation miss more shifts.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
=B. Unreliable transportation can make workers miss scheduled shifts.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~C. Transportation has no effect on work schedules.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source note.
~D. Workers should avoid all transportation.#Incorrect. This adds an unsupported idea.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q28::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q28
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.V.01
~A. The thing is good because stuff happens.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
~B. Evidence is a word in the presentation.#Incorrect. This does not use academic vocabulary meaningfully.
=C. The evidence is credible because it comes from a local workforce report.#Correct. Credible is used accurately.
~D. The claim is claimy and has evidence things.#Incorrect. This is not academic language.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q29::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q29
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.R.10
Source note: Clear emergency communication helps residents make informed safety decisions. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A.  Emergency communication prevents all danger.#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
=B.  Clear emergency messages can help residents make informed safety choices.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
~C.  Residents never use emergency communication.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
~D.  Safety decisions are unrelated to communication.#Incorrect. The source connects them.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q30::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q30
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.R.12
~A.  It distracts from the point.#Incorrect. The repetition supports the point.
=B.  It emphasizes the qualities the speaker wants the audience to remember.#Correct. Repetition makes the key idea memorable.
~C.  It replaces all evidence.#Incorrect. Rhetoric should not replace evidence.
~D.  It makes the claim unrelated.#Incorrect. The repetition reinforces the claim.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q31::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q31
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
Which transition best introduces evidence in an oral response? {
~A.  In conclusion,#Incorrect. This signals ending, not evidence.
~B.  On a totally different topic,#Incorrect. This signals a shift away from the focus.
=C.  According to the report,#Correct. This introduces source-based evidence.
~D.  Anyway,#Incorrect. This is informal and unfocused.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q32::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q32
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.V.01
Which sentence uses academic vocabulary precisely? {
~A.  The evidence thing is good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B.  The claim has stuff.#Incorrect. This is informal.
~C.  The report is report-like.#Incorrect. This is not precise.
=D.  The evidence supports the claim by showing a measurable pattern.#Correct. Supports, claim, and measurable are used precisely.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q33::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q33
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.L.01
Which revision fixes the sentence? Draft: The focus is clear it helps listeners follow the evidence. {
=A.  The focus is clear, and it helps listeners follow the evidence.#Correct. This correctly joins two complete ideas.
~B.  The focus is clear it helps listeners follow the evidence.#Incorrect. This is a run-on.
~C.  Focus clear helps listeners evidence.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
~D.  Because the focus clear.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q34::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q34
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
Which response best explains evidence after presenting it? {
~A.  That is evidence.#Incorrect. This labels but does not explain.
=B.  This matters because missed shifts can reduce income and job stability.#Correct. This explains why the evidence matters.
~C.  Moving to a new unrelated topic.#Incorrect. This does not explain evidence.
~D.  Evidence is evidence.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q35::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q35
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.C.01
Which oral response problem is most serious? {
~A.  The speaker uses one clear transition.#Incorrect. A transition helps.
~B.  The speaker explains why evidence matters.#Incorrect. Explanation is useful.
=C.  The speaker lists evidence but never states a focus.#Correct. Without focus, listeners cannot organize the evidence.
~D.  The speaker ends with a conclusion.#Incorrect. A conclusion can help.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q36::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q36
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.L.01
~A. Evidence supports claim because report.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
=B. The evidence supports the claim because it comes from a credible report.#Correct. This is clear and conventional.
~C. The evidence supports the claim it comes from report.#Incorrect. This is a run-on sentence.
~D. Because credible report evidence.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q37::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q37
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.V.01
Which explanation uses academic vocabulary precisely? {
~A.  The speaker says words in a speech.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B.  The speech is speechy and good.#Incorrect. This is not academic language.
~C.  The device is a thing.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=D.  The speaker's parallel structure emphasizes three related claims.#Correct. This uses parallel structure and emphasizes accurately.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q38::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q38
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.R.10
Source note: The study found that tutoring increased course completion rates. Which paraphrase is accurate? {
~A.  Tutoring lowered course completion rates.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B.  The study ignored tutoring.#Incorrect. Tutoring is the focus.
~C.  Course completion was unrelated to support.#Incorrect. The source note connects them.
=D.  The study found that students completed courses more often when tutoring was available.#Correct. This preserves the meaning.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q39::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q39
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.V.01
What does concise mean in oral communication? {
=A.  brief, clear, and focused.#Correct. Concise speech avoids unnecessary wording.
~B.  confusing and long.#Incorrect. Concise means brief and clear.
~C.  unrelated to the topic.#Incorrect. Concise communication stays focused.
~D.  informal and vague.#Incorrect. Concise does not mean informal.
}

::ENG4_U06_UA_Q40::
Question ID: ENG4_U06_UA_Q40
MLA Standard: MLA.ENG4.L.01
Which sentence is ready for a formal oral presentation? {
~A.  The report says stuff about transit.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=B.  The report explains how transit reliability affects worker attendance.#Correct. This is clear and formal.
~C.  Transit stuff is in the report.#Incorrect. This is informal.
~D.  Report transit worker attendance affects.#Incorrect. This is not standard English.
}
